WEBVTT

00:00:00.400 --> 00:00:07.105
Polar coordinates locate a point using a directed distance from the origin and an angle

00:00:07.105 --> 00:00:12.022
For nonnegative radius, move outward along the ray at that angle

00:00:12.022 --> 00:00:17.833
Multiplying the unit-circle coordinates by the radius converts the location into Cartesian coordinates.

00:00:19.133 --> 00:00:25.603
At radius two and angle sixty degrees, the horizontal coordinate is two times one half, giving one

00:00:25.603 --> 00:00:32.833
The vertical coordinate is two times the square root of three over two, giving the square root of three.

00:00:34.133 --> 00:00:36.291
Polar names are not unique

00:00:36.291 --> 00:00:44.492
Adding a complete turn keeps the same radius and final ray, so the same point receives another angle label

00:00:44.492 --> 00:00:50.967
This differs from ordinary Cartesian coordinates, where each ordered pair names one fixed coordinate combination.

00:00:52.267 --> 00:00:56.472
A negative radius reverses the direction along the chosen ray

00:00:56.472 --> 00:01:01.938
Changing the radius sign while adding half a turn names the same point

00:01:01.938 --> 00:01:08.667
When converting back from coordinates, use quadrant information instead of relying on a tangent ratio alone.
